mode: "100644"
text: "# TikTok Photo Mode 2026 — verified\n\n*Volatile. Re-verify quarterly (algorithm weighting + format limits move).*\n\n## The honest tension (don't oversell it)\nTwo credible 2026 data stories coexist:\n- **Bullish:** Photo Mode is in a growth-phase distribution boost; some accounts report large reach jumps;\n  carousels can **match or beat video on likes/comments**; the FYP surfaces them to non-followers; the\n  format is **less saturated** than video.\n- **Skeptical (Metricool 2026 TikTok Study):** TikTok is still **video-first** — video gets **~5.6× more\n  views** and **~7.8× more interactions** than image/carousel; image/carousel grew **140% YoY** but\n  **per-post views fell ~23%** and **interactions fell ~62%** as users skip them more. Yet a handful of\n  beauty/fitness/education accounts pull **100–200× their size-category engagement** with carousels.\n\n**Synthesis (the honest stance):** Photo Mode is a **high-ceiling, niche-dependent format that wins when\nit earns completion** — not a guaranteed reach hack. It can punch far above its weight for the right\ncontent type and niche; forced onto the wrong content it underperforms video. **Never promise virality or\na \"5×\".**\n\n## The signal that matters: completion rate\nTikTok treats Photo Mode **like video** in recommendation. **Completion rate** (what % swipe to slide 2+,\nand through the end) is the **primary signal**. Swiping = active engagement; dwell time is tracked even\nthough slides are static. So **slide 1 and the last slide matter most**, and every slide must earn the next.\n\n## Format + design (verify)\n- **9:16 vertical, 1080×1920** — NOT Instagram's 4:5/square (square leaves dead space and kills\n  engagement). **Design for TikTok first, then crop to 4:5 for IG.**\n- **Safe zone:** keep key content **centred** — the right side holds like/comment/share, the bottom holds\n  the caption, the top-left the profile.\n- **5–7 slides** is the sweet spot (native max **35**); shorter, punchier wins (\"5 steps\" not 15).\n- **Native Photo Mode beats an MP4 \"slideshow\"** — uploaded as a native photo post it's treated\n  differently and gets the best organic reach. (WoopSocial's `postType=PHOTO` posts native photo mode.)\n\n## Structure for completion\n**Slide 1 = the thumbnail/hook** (bold visual or text-overlay question/claim; promise a payoff). Then\n**progressive reveal** (each slide adds new info, no repeats), **cliffhangers between slides** (\"the worst\none is next\", \"but here's the thing\"), and a **reward at the end** (best insight/punchline) + **one CTA**.\nIf you promise \"the result is on slide 5,\" deliver it.\n\n## Sound, text, hashtags, tone\n- **Sound matters even for photos** — a track boosts distribution (ambient/lo-fi for educational, trending\n  for discovery). Maps to the API `autoAddMusic` field.\n- **Text overlays are indexed by TikTok search** (TikTok SEO) — slides surface in keyword searches; on-screen\n  text, captions, and hashtags all carry weight. **1–2 hashtags, never >5**; skip #fyp/#viral (uninformative).\n- **Tone:** authenticity/personality > polished/corporate; photo dumps feel native on TikTok (vs low-effort\n  on IG). Don't just cross-post an Instagram carousel.\n\n## Winning content types + engagement tactics\nListicles (\"7 tools…\"), before/after, step tutorials (one step/slide), hot takes/unpopular opinions\n(drive comments), photo/aesthetic dumps, story arcs (\"how I went from X to Y\"), myth-busting. **Question in\nthe caption ≈ +26% comments**; asking for a comment ≈ **+14%**, but **asking for a like ≈ −60% likes**;\nfirst-hour comments boost reach; **~96% of reach happens in the first 10 days.**\n"
